SET ECHO OFF
STOP_ON_ERROR OFF

ECHO === TWO CURSORS, ONE FILE: can a buffered COMMIT overwrite a newer write? ===
ECHO SINGLE-PROCESS proxy for two engines on one data directory. It tests the
ECHO COMMIT PATH's staleness behaviour, which is the same code two processes
ECHO reach. It does NOT test OS record locking -- that needs two processes.
ECHO
ECHO v2. The first cut assumed 1-based areas and a bare second USE. The engine
ECHO corrected both: areas are 0-BASED, and a duplicate open is REFUSED unless
ECHO AGAIN is given -- "two record pointers, not one moved by SELECT", which is
ECHO exactly the instrument this probe needs.
ECHO
ECHO v3. Reads go through SMARTLIST, not LIST. OWNER RULING 2026-09-19: LIST is
ECHO the owner's own MANUAL tool -- the caller controls the cursors -- and
ECHO SMARTLIST is the official listing command. A probe in the corpus models a
ECHO habit whether it means to or not, so it models the official one. SMARTLIST
ECHO declares "mutates: cursor"; every REPLACE below is already preceded by its
ECHO own GO 1, so nothing here depends on where a read left the pointer.

SET PATH DBF DBF/SANDBOX

ECHO --- fixture, in area 0 ---
ERASE TWOWRITE CONFIRM
CREATE X64 TWOWRITE (ID C(4), F1 C(8), F2 C(8))
USE TWOWRITE
APPEND BLANK
REPLACE ID WITH "R1"
REPLACE F1 WITH "ORIG1"
REPLACE F2 WITH "ORIG2"
GO 1
SMARTLIST 1

ECHO --- second independent cursor in area 1 ---
SELECT 1
USE TWOWRITE AGAIN ALIAS T2
AREA
SMARTLIST 1

ECHO --- PROBE A: T2 buffers a change to F1 and does NOT commit ---
TABLE ON
GO 1
REPLACE F1 WITH "FROM_T2"
ECHO TUP and SMARTLIST both read through the area; compare them:
TUP
SMARTLIST 1

ECHO --- PROBE B: area 0 writes F1 IMMEDIATELY ---
SELECT 0
TABLE OFF
GO 1
REPLACE F1 WITH "FROM_A0"
SMARTLIST 1

ECHO --- PROBE C: now T2 commits its STALE buffer ---
SELECT T2
COMMIT
SELECT 0
GO 1
ECHO F1 = FROM_T2 means the stale buffered commit OVERWROTE a newer write with
ECHO no complaint. F1 = FROM_A0 means something detected or refused it.
SMARTLIST 1

ECHO --- PROBE D: the per-field question. Nobody buffered F2. ---
ECHO F2 = ORIG2 means COMMIT writes ONLY the fields it buffered, so two editors
ECHO on DIFFERENT fields of one record both survive.
TUP

ECHO --- teardown, and it exercises the new bare-USE close ---
SELECT T2
USE
SELECT 0
USE
ERASE TWOWRITE CONFIRM
ECHO === probe done ===
